  "theory": {
"intro": "The **natural logarithm** $\\ln x$ is the logarithm with base $e$, so $\\ln x = \\log_e x$. It undoes the exponential: $\\ln(e^x)=x$ and $e^{\\ln x}=x$ for $x>0$. Every logarithm law applies to $\\ln x$.",
"facts": [
"Definition: $\\ln x = \\log_e x$, defined for $x > 0$.",
"Inverse identities: $\\ln(e^x) = x$ for all real $x$, and $e^{\\ln x} = x$ for $x > 0$.",
"Special values: $\\ln 1 = 0$ because $e^0 = 1$, and $\\ln e = 1$ because $e^1 = e$.",
"Powers of $e$: $\\ln(e^k) = k\\ln e = k$, so $\\ln(\\sqrt{e}) = \\ln(e^{1/2}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.",
"Log laws for $a,b>0$ and real $k$: $\\ln(ab) = \\ln a + \\ln b$, $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b$ and $\\ln(a^k) = k\\ln a$.",
"Conversion: $y=e^x$ says the same thing as $x=\\ln y$, for $y>0$.",
"Common error: the equations $\\ln(a+b) = \\ln a + \\ln b$ and $\\dfrac{\\ln a}{\\ln b} = \\ln(a-b)$ are not valid identities in general, even where both sides are defined."
],
